# 0001. Adopt Agent-legible Repository Harness

Status: Accepted

## Context

The project needs repeatable local decisions for agent guidance, docs layout, local and full validation, runtime smoke checks, generated artifacts, and architecture boundaries.

Without a repository-local harness, the same preferences get rediscovered in chat or reconstructed in ad hoc scripts. Agent-generated projects especially need early decisions for layer direction, DTO ownership, maintainability limits, and which checks are active now versus deferred until the stack exists.

## Decision

Use a short `AGENTS.md`, a progressive-disclosure `docs/` tree, and check-only scripts under `scripts/`.

The core harness files are:

- `AGENTS.md`
- `docs/README.md`
- `docs/SPEC_TEMPLATE.md`
- `docs/TRACEABILITY_GUIDE.md`
- `docs/research/engineering-principles.md`
- `docs/research/hci-principles.md`
- `docs/specs/agent-governance-v0.1.md`
- `docs/specs/architecture-layering-v0.1.md`
- `docs/tasks/alignment-check.md`
- `docs/tasks/scaffold-planning.md`
- `docs/tasks/e2e-testing.md`
- `scripts/validate-changed-scope.mjs`
- `scripts/validate-traceability-structure.mjs`
- `scripts/finalize.sh`
- `scripts/dev.sh`
- `scripts/smoke.sh`

## Consequences

- Durable project knowledge belongs in repository docs, not only chat history.
- Repeated conventions should become mechanical checks when practical.
- `validate-changed-scope.mjs` is the mandatory local completion gate.
- Non-trivial behavior changes should preserve requirement/example/acceptance traceability.
- `finalize.sh` is the CI/pre-merge full gate and must remain check-only.
- Runtime smoke checks stay separate from finalization because they start services and require free ports.
- Some rules should be documented before scaffolding but mechanically enabled only after their target stack exists.
